## Why Does This Systematically Destroy Any Exploitation Window?

Recalling that our final theoretical point of entry was a physical silicon vulnerability (such as harvesting memory states through side-channels or electrical trace probing), the RAM-sharded VM model completely neutralizes this boundary for three core reasons:

1. **Impossibility of Memory Reverse-Engineering:** Even if an adversary deploys a military-grade hardware exploit to dump volatile RAM contents, they will harvest nothing but non-contiguous, scrambled, and encrypted binary shards. Without access to the secure Tracker map—which alters its indexing vectors every nanosecond—this data is mathematically useless. It is equivalent to trying to read a textbook shredded into 10,000 random confetti pieces scattered into the wind.

2. **Extreme Moving Target Defense (MTD):** The memory fragments of the running VM are never stationary. The low-level hypervisor executes a continuous Runtime Memory ASLR routine, completely shuffling the physical addresses of the 4KB shards thousands of times per second.

3. **Immortal Self-Healing Redundancy:** If a single 4KB shard in RAM experiences corruption due to physical radiation or a hardware fault-injection attempt (such as motherboard voltage glitching), the consensus tracker automatically flags the invalid cryptographic signature of that specific slice. The CPU discards the corrupted fragment immediately, extracts a pristine, unmutated copy from the physical secure ROM, and reconstructs the memory shard in an entirely new, randomized RAM address within milliseconds, maintaining continuous software uptime.

